        "| Does lower-duplication evaluation rescue semantic risk control? | No on valid Llama unique32. v97's best delta is `+0.014` with no CI-positive policy; the Gemma unique16 packet JSONL is unusable/empty and must be rebuilt before packet-level scoring. | [v97](css_research_note_v97_unique_source_semantic_risk.md) |",
        "| Does rebuilding Gemma unique-source rescue semantic risk control? | No. v98 rebuilds Gemma unique16 cleanly, runs 81 lower-duplication semantic-risk policies, and still finds zero CI-positive policies; best point estimate is `+0.051` with `3/30` baseline regressions, while the best clean row is only `+0.035` with CI low `+0.000`. | [v98](css_research_note_v98_rebuilt_unique_semantic_risk.md) |",
        "| Is the raw semantic scorer completely dead on lower-duplication targets? | No. v99 target-thresholds raw scores and finds `4/81` lower-CI-positive oracle rows; best is `+0.063` with `5/36` recoveries and `30/30` baseline preservation. This is target-oracle calibration headroom, not deployable evidence. | [v99](css_research_note_v99_raw_semantic_threshold_boundary.md) |",
        "| Does target-threshold headroom survive source split training? | Yes. v100 repeats the audit under the v98 fit/calibration split and finds `5/81` target-oracle positive rows; best is `+0.065` with `7/36` recoveries. Source thresholds still fail baseline-risk transfer. | [v100](css_research_note_v100_split_trained_semantic_threshold_audit.md) |",
        "| Does more source calibration make semantic scoring deployable? | No. v101 sweeps 72 runs / 1080 source-threshold rows over calibration size, source composition, features, seeds, and packet-vs-problem-disjoint splits. Zero source-calibrated rows pass; problem-disjoint clean source rows are all no-op, while target-oracle headroom remains small but real. | [v101](css_research_note_v101_semantic_calibration_scaling.md) |",
        "| Does labeled target-style calibration rescue semantic scoring? | Not at this panel size. v102 trains on source packets, calibrates thresholds on target-style labeled packets, and evaluates held-out target packets over 72 runs / 1080 rows. Zero rows pass; packet-disjoint has one tiny clean point-positive row, and problem-disjoint clean rows are all no-op. | [v102](css_research_note_v102_target_style_semantic_calibration.md) |",
        "| Does a larger labeled target-style panel rescue semantic scoring? | Still no as a conservative policy. v103 expands Gemma target calibration to 48 packets/category and runs 756 Llama-to-Gemma threshold rows. Zero target-calibrated rows pass; best clean calibrated gain is only `+0.007` with zero lower bound, while held-out oracle thresholding reaches `+0.034` with positive lower bound. | [v103](css_research_note_v103_expanded_target_semantic_calibration.md) |",
        "| Does richer hashed semantic evidence rescue target calibration? | No. v104 adds problem text, more representatives, longer rationales, larger hash space, and longer training on the expanded Gemma target. Conservative target-calibrated behavior is unchanged: zero CI-positive rows and best clean gain `+0.007`; oracle rows become more active but remain diagnostic and can regress baselines. | [v104](css_research_note_v104_rich_signal_semantic_calibration.md) |",
        "| Does a multifeature semantic accept/fallback gate rescue calibration? | No. v105 trains target-style utility gates over v103/v104 raw predictions. Zero target-calibrated rows pass; best clean gate is only `+0.008` with zero lower bound, while the best active point estimate regresses baseline and remains CI-negative. | [v105](css_research_note_v105_semantic_meta_gate.md) |",
        "| Do cheap symbolic/process features rescue calibration? | No. v106 trains answer-shape, baseline-equivalence, and arithmetic-consistency features over Llama unique-source packets and evaluates on expanded Gemma. Best clean calibrated rows recover only `1` held-out packet with zero lower bound, and active raw rows regress baseline while still missing top20-only tails. | [v106](css_research_note_v106_symbolic_feature_boundary.md) |",
        "| Do representative-level process/proof-hygiene features rescue calibration? | No. v107 trains a low-dimensional process cluster scorer over support/rank, arithmetic consistency, final-answer hygiene, and representative-process features. Llama->Gemma has tiny point-positive rows, but all seeds/directions/overlap regimes have lower CI at zero or below, so zero policies pass. | [v107](css_research_note_v107_process_cluster_scorer.md) |",
        "| Can cross-generator agreement replace a verifier? | Not conservatively. v108 shows Llama as an auxiliary trace helps Gemma (`target_intersection_top10` mean `+0.042` with 81 recoveries/6 regressions), but Gemma hurts Llama and no positive no-regression policy appears. | [v108](css_research_note_v108_cross_generator_agreement.md) |",
        "| Can cross-generator agreement be risk-gated? | Yes, narrowly. v109 calibrates auxiliary-trace policies and finds Gemma-with-Llama `union_rank_top3` is robust at 24/36 calibration problems (`+0.102`/`+0.126`, 3/3 CI-positive seeds), while Llama-with-Gemma remains unsafe or flat. | [v109](css_research_note_v109_cross_generator_risk_gate.md) |",
        "| Does the generator router transfer across seeds? | Yes for the same narrow branch. v110 trains and thresholds on two source seeds, then deploys on the held-out seed; Gemma-with-Llama `pool_all` gets `+0.084` mean delta with 3/3 CI-positive seeds, 152 recoveries, and 2 regressions. | [v110](css_research_note_v110_cross_seed_generator_router.md) |",
        "| Is the cross-seed router just threshold luck? | Unlikely. v111 permutes source utility labels 200 times; zero placebo runs match the observed Gemma-with-Llama deltas, with `pool_all` placebo max `+0.030` vs observed `+0.084`. | [v111](css_research_note_v111_cross_seed_router_placebo.md) |",
        "| Do dumb auxiliary heuristics match the learned router? | No under valid tie-safe thresholding. v112 makes rank/prior heuristics no-op and the best support/confidence rows reach only `+0.054` vs v110 `+0.084`. | [v112](css_research_note_v112_cross_seed_router_heuristic_ablation.md) |",
        "| What if regression budget is explicit instead of zero-source-regression? | Learned routing still gives the best deployable frontier. v113 source-selected Gemma-with-Llama reaches `+0.119` with 3 held-out regressions at source budget 2; the best <=5-regression heuristic is candidate-ratio at `+0.084`. | [v113](css_research_note_v113_cross_seed_router_regression_frontier.md) |",
        "| Does that router survive problem-disjoint source calibration? | Only as recovery signal, not as safe calibration. v114 removes source rows whose problem ids appear in the held-out seed; learned rows stay positive (`+0.097` at source budget 0, `+0.118` at budget 2) but held-out regressions jump to 20/30, so low-regression calibration fails. | [v114](css_research_note_v114_problem_disjoint_router_frontier.md) |",
        "| Does a same-feature correctness head fix problem-disjoint regression risk? | No. v115 trains a candidate-correctness head on all changed problem-disjoint source rows, but no row gets <=5 held-out regressions; the two-head variants mostly reshuffle v114 rather than making it safe. | [v115](css_research_note_v115_problem_disjoint_two_head_router.md) |",
        "| Are current scores totally unable to separate recoveries from regressions? | Not totally. v116 finds moderate recovery-vs-regression AUC around `0.70` for utility/correctness heads, but weak-fold and tail overlap are enough to break low-regression threshold transfer. | [v116](css_research_note_v116_problem_disjoint_separability.md) |",
        "| Do local traces contain logprobs or hidden-state signals for the next router? | No. v117 audits all four local Monkey Business JSONs: samples are plain strings with correctness labels; there are no logprobs, hidden states, embeddings, confidence, finish reasons, or decoder telemetry. | [v117](css_research_note_v117_trace_signal_availability.md) |",
        "| Can answer-shape features guard problem-disjoint auxiliary-router regressions? | No. v118 answer-symbolic features trail the original base utility score (`0.608` vs `0.711` recovery-vs-regression AUC for base+symbolic utility vs base utility) and still produce no <=5-regression aggregate row. | [v118](css_research_note_v118_answer_symbolic_guard.md) |",
        "| Does a narrower pairwise verifier interface help? | Promising smoke. v119 asks local models to choose between baseline answer A and auxiliary candidate B on accepted router rows: mathstral/gemma4 recover `9/20` and `10/20` with `0/20` accepted regressions; qwen3:14b recovers `15/20` with `3/20` regressions. | [v119](css_research_note_v119_pairwise_router_judge.md) |",
        "| Does the pairwise signal survive the full accepted action set? | Yes as accepted-row evidence. v120 scales to all 377 accepted budget-0 Gemma-with-Llama actions: mathstral recovers `105/192` with `0/20` regressions, gemma4 `111/192` with `0/20`, and qwen3:14b `137/192` with `3/20`. | [v120](css_research_note_v120_full_pairwise_router_judge.md) |",
        "| Can pairwise judge choice be calibrated on source accepted rows? | Yes with a caveat. v121 selects model/rule on source rows excluding held-out problem ids and gets `+0.368` mean accepted-row delta, `120` recoveries, and `1` regression over held-out seeds. | [v121](css_research_note_v121_pairwise_router_judge_calibration.md) |",
        "| What is the natural-rate impact of pairwise-gated routing? | Positive and regression-reduced. v122 maps the v121 policy back to all `1776` held-out Gemma trials: raw router delta `+0.097` with `192/20` rec/reg, pairwise-gated delta `+0.067` with `120/1`. | [v122](css_research_note_v122_pairwise_natural_rate.md) |",
        "| Does the same pairwise machinery fabricate a reverse-direction win? | No. v123 Llama-with-Gemma has bad raw action supply (`1/5` rec/reg), raw natural delta `-0.002`, and source-calibrated pairwise selection chooses no-op on every fold. | [v123](css_research_note_v123_pairwise_mirror_control.md) |",
        "| Is the v122 natural gain concentrated in one problem? | No in leave-one-problem-out. v124 drops each `(seed,pid)` group: natural pairwise delta remains positive in `222/222` cases, min `+0.063`, and the lone regression is localized to `s60601/p88/t0`. | [v124](css_research_note_v124_pairwise_sensitivity.md) |",
        "| Does pairwise gating still help with a higher-risk upstream router? | Yes. v125 raises the raw-router source budget to `2`: raw natural delta is `+0.118` with `240/30` rec/reg; pairwise budget1 gets `+0.083` with `150/2`, and pairwise budget2 gets `+0.099` with `180/4`. | [v125](css_research_note_v125_pairwise_budget2_frontier.md) |",
        "| Are the v125 higher-budget gains concentrated in one problem? | No. v126 leave-one-problem-out keeps budget1 and budget2 natural deltas positive for `222/222` groups, with min `+0.079` and `+0.095`; budget2 regressions are four `qwen14b/B` choices on `union_rank_top3`. | [v126](css_research_note_v126_pairwise_budget2_sensitivity.md) |",
        "| Is the higher-budget pairwise row just a budget cherry-pick? | No. v127 sweeps pairwise source budgets: the guarded rule set plateaus at `+0.099` from budget `2` through `30`, while an unsafe `always` rule restores raw-router regressions. | [v127](css_research_note_v127_pairwise_budget_curve.md) |",
        "| Can a deployable guard reduce the budget2 qwen/union tail risk? | Partly. v128 fixed qwen/union cross-judge confirmation gets `+0.083` with `149/1`; source-selected guard budget1 gets `+0.086` with `155/2`. This is a safety frontier, not a solved verifier. | [v128](css_research_note_v128_pairwise_guard_sweep.md) |",
        "| Is the budget2 increment broad, and can simple policy-family guards rescue it? | The increment is real but sparse: budget2 adds `+28` correct trials (`+0.016`) over budget1 and stays LOO-positive for `222/222` groups, but only `17/222` groups are nonzero. Source-disjoint policy-family guards do not improve v125. | [v129](css_research_note_v129_pairwise_budget_increment_guard.md) |",
        "| Do richer pairwise prompts eliminate the localized qwen/union regression tail? | No. v130 type-check prompting fixes repeated `p88` digit-cycle regressions and preserves matched `p63` recoveries, but `p82` weekday regressions remain; mathstral loses recoveries and gemma4 is structurally invalid. | [v130](css_research_note_v130_pairwise_rich_prompt_probe.md) |",
